retired Admiral sandy Winnefeld is a CBS  News military and homeland security  analyst he was vice chairman of the  Joint Chiefs of Staff when secretary  mattis led the US Central Command  Admiral good morning good morning John  and well what do you make of secretary  mattis's letter well first of all of all  of the people who have left or are about  to leave this administration this is  probably the most concerning not only  did Jim mattis get to the department on  a good trajectory to restore its  readiness and lethality in the face of  all threats he also helped buttress our  allies at a very difficult time for  relations with allies and perhaps most  importantly when all those national  security decisions were being made in  the White House we could all take  comfort that well at least Jim mattis is  in the room and he's not going to be in  that room much longer as his letter  points out he had some fundamental  disagreements with the president and as  the old adage says either support your  boss or resign and he chose to resign  before he got fired and Merle you know  secretary mattis what do you make though  of the fact that he made such a broad  and sweeping set of statements in this  letter secretary Madison is it knows the  chain of command he knows what what  you're supposed to do and so how do you  frame it with respect to what you know  about secretary mattis well I think that  secretary mattis has been working on  this letter for a while clearly there  was a lot of tension in the relationship  beginning probably all the way back to  the withdrawal from the Iran nuclear  agreement and a whole host of other  challenges as well whether it was  transgender personnel in the military or  a parade down Pennsylvania Avenue or  trips to the southern border I think  that this was building up and in  Jim Madison's mind and his letter uses  integrity Jim mattis is nothing if not a  man of integrity and I think it's a very  important letter that will go down in  the history of this nation as you  mentioned earlier a historian said this  is the first time a Secretary of Defense  has resigned so it's clear that pulling  out of Syria was the final straw for  general mattis let me ask you about some  unintended consequences from that  decision because one headline that came  shortly after came from the Kurds who  said that because of that they would  have to release some 32  Isis prisoners which would be  devastating for the region but also for  Europe what is your reaction to these  unintended consequences well there is an  awful lot swirling around the whole  Syria situation not the least of which  is the brutal regime of Bashar al-assad  and Iran and Russia which support him  but also the potential for a return to  instability in eastern Syria and in fact  western Iraq so it is a very bitter pill  to swallow to more or less abandon the  Kurds who have done most of the fighting  out there against ISIL and all those  unintended consequences you talk about  release of prisoners less humanitarian  aid being delivered to people in that  region we're gonna feel the effects for  quite some time sandy I'm finished I'm  struck by the phrase you just said loses  integrity what are the broader  implications for morale quickly well I  think that the US military will look at  this with a little bit of shock and  dismay but I would also tell you there's  a lot of really good leadership still  residing in the Pentagon we've got a  great deputy secretary we've got a  terrific chairman and I think that the  Department will be fine but it is a  little bit of a jolt to the system to be  sure all right Admiral Winnefeld thanks  so much for being with us
 

